  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>Fréjus's large wild beach, lined by a dune strip, includes a naturist zone open from June to September and an area open to dogs on a leash, near the Argens lifeguard post. The tourist office presents it as the "Saint-Aygulf beach for dogs" section, on Les Esclamandes beach.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>The town of Fréjus and its tourist office describe this area without mentioning any time or seasonal restriction: dogs must be kept on a leash there. The beaches are supervised every day from the first Friday of June to the third Sunday of September, with a post at the Argens. We found no official source on facilities (showers, bags, shade): bring water, a sunshade and poop bags.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: the Villepey ponds right behind are suitable for a walk on a leash; swimming is banned there according to Le Var Tourisme.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p><a href="/en/fiche-plage-pacha-frejus">Plage du Pacha</a>, Fréjus's other dog beach, is at the far end of the Base Nature — but not reachable on foot from here, as the Argens river mouth separates the two banks.</p>
    </div>

